   <dt>Movement</dt><dd>De Stijl</dd>
   <dt>Medium</dt><dd>Oil on canvas</dd>
   <dt>Collection</dt><dd>Kunsthaus Zurich</dd>
  </dl>
  <p style="margin-top:.8rem">Also listed as <em>Composition II in Red, Blue, and Yellow</em>.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="why">
  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>Its asymmetric balance of a few primary-colour blocks became a reference for modern graphic design and architecture.</p>
  <h2 style="margin-top:1.2rem">De Stijl</h2>
  <p>Founded in the Netherlands in 1917 by Theo van Doesburg and Piet Mondrian. Its Neoplasticism allows:</p>
  <ul>
   <li>only horizontal and vertical lines</li>
   <li>the primary colours red, blue and yellow</li>
   <li>the non-colours white, grey and black</li>
  </ul>
 </section>
